1049:## Explanatory Patterns of Apartment Unit-Area Sale Prices in Seoul Using XGBoost and SHAP
1051:This study analyzes explanatory patterns of apartment **unit-area sale prices** in Seoul by integrating machine learning with eXplainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI). While the traditional Hedonic Price Model based on OLS regression has limited ability to capture nonlinear relationships, machine learning models suffer from a lack of interpretability. Furthermore, using total transaction price as the dependent variable causes floor area to dominate SHAP rankings, obscuring qualitative and locational signals. To address this, this study adopts **log(price per square meter)** as the dependent variable, normalizing scale effects into the denominator and revealing latent quality-based explanatory structure.
1053:391,826 apartment transactions across 215 administrative districts (haengjeong-dong) of Seoul, covering contracts from January 2019 to December 2025, were collected from public data sources. Observations from the second half of 2025 should be interpreted as provisional because reporting delays may still remain. Eighteen independent variables spanning physical, locational, environmental, and macroeconomic characteristics were constructed. A spatial mapping methodology combining Nominatim geocoding and GeoJSON spatial joins was developed to align legal-district-based transaction data with an administrative-district analytical framework. Station/school/department-store/CCTV/park/library variables were collected as a single stock snapshot and merged to all transactions, representing a time-consistent but not year-specific measurement.
1055:Three models — OLS, Random Forest, and XGBoost — were compared under three split schemes with identical hyperparameters. Under the random split, XGBoost achieved **R²_log = 0.9554 (Median APE = 4.16%)**, outperforming OLS (R²_log = 0.5061) and Random Forest (R²_log = 0.8937). To assess generalization beyond same-complex repeated transactions, an additional GroupShuffleSplit on 8,601 complexes (legal-dong + apartment name, overlap = 0) and a chronological split (test: July 2024 ~ Dec 2025, 113,469 transactions) were performed. XGBoost yielded R²_log = 0.8005 (Median APE = 11.72%) under the Group split and R²_log = 0.7972 (Median APE = 12.61%) under the chronological split. Since these two values are nearly identical, same-complex repeated-transaction leakage accounts for the majority of the drop from the random-split score, while the 2024–2025 market-regime shift contributes marginally. In all three splits, the tree-based models consistently outperformed OLS (R²_log ≈ 0.39–0.50) by a wide margin.
1057:SHAP analysis applied to the XGBoost model showed a substantially **reordered importance ranking after unit-area normalization**: building age (12.43%), Gangnam location (11.74%), childcare centers per dong (11.53%), M2 money supply (10.77%), private academies per dong (9.61%), and floor area (9.55%). Floor area, which dominated total-price SHAP rankings (22.2% as first), drops to sixth place under unit-price normalization, allowing latent qualitative signals — particularly residential-neighborhood features (childcare, academies, building age) — to emerge. The SHAP dependence plot for building age revealed a clear U-shape in the 25–30+ year range, capturing redevelopment-expectation effects that OLS linear specifications cannot represent.
1059:Three separate regional models (overall / Gangnam-3-gu / non-Gangnam) were estimated with the Gangnam dummy removed (17 variables), directly addressing the reviewer-proposed shift from year-based to region-based disaggregation. **Gangnam-3-gu** (n=65,077, XGBoost R²_log = 0.9356) was dominated by building age (16.46%), CPI (13.25%), and department-store count (13.04%), indicating that redevelopment expectations and commercial agglomeration drive unit-price structure. **Non-Gangnam** (n=326,749, R²_log = 0.9421) showed building age (14.81%), CPI (11.79%), floor area (11.19%), childcare centers (10.57%), and private academies (8.63%) in its top five — foregrounding residential-neighborhood and education-infrastructure signals. Childcare centers were bottom-ranked in Gangnam but fourth in non-Gangnam, demonstrating qualitatively distinct unit-price determinants across regions. Moran's I residual diagnostics showed that OLS exhibits strong positive spatial autocorrelation (I = 0.325, p < 0.001), while XGBoost residuals are effectively uncorrelated (I = 0.004, p = 0.363), confirming that the tree-based nonlinear model absorbs spatial structure even without explicit spatial-econometric controls. An ablation removing private academies and childcare centers reduced XGBoost R²_log by only 0.0004, indicating that the district-level proxy variables do not materially distort the overall predictive structure, although their SHAP contributions should still be read as neighborhood-level coarse signals rather than precise facility-location effects.
1061:This study contributes to the literature by (i) systematically applying XAI to the Korean apartment market with a unit-area-normalized dependent variable, (ii) directly implementing region-separated models to reveal qualitatively distinct unit-price structures between Gangnam and non-Gangnam areas, and (iii) presenting an integrated empirical design that combines predictive performance comparison across three split schemes with interpretable SHAP decomposition.
1063:**Keywords:** Apartment unit-area sale price, XGBoost, SHAP, Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI), Hedonic Price Model, Seoul real estate market, administrative district analysis, region-separated models

