./paper/논문_초안_v7_m2price.md:1059:Three separate regional models (overall / Gangnam-3-gu / non-Gangnam) were estimated with the Gangnam dummy removed (17 variables), directly addressing the reviewer-proposed shift from year-based to region-based disaggregation. **Gangnam-3-gu** (n=65,077, XGBoost R²_log = 0.9356) was dominated by building age (16.46%), CPI (13.25%), and department-store count (13.04%), indicating that redevelopment expectations and commercial agglomeration drive unit-price structure. **Non-Gangnam** (n=326,749, R²_log = 0.9421) showed building age (14.81%), CPI (11.79%), floor area (11.19%), childcare centers (10.57%), and private academies (8.63%) in its top five — foregrounding residential-neighborhood and education-infrastructure signals. Childcare centers were bottom-ranked in Gangnam but fourth in non-Gangnam, demonstrating qualitatively distinct unit-price determinants across regions. Moran's I residual diagnostics showed that OLS exhibits strong positive spatial autocorrelation (I = 0.325, p < 0.001), while XGBoost residuals are effectively uncorrelated (I = 0.004, p = 0.363), confirming that the tree-based nonlinear model absorbs spatial structure even without explicit spatial-econometric controls. An ablation removing private academies and childcare centers reduced XGBoost R²_log by only 0.0004, indicating that the district-level proxy variables do not materially distort the overall predictive structure, although their SHAP contributions should still be read as neighborhood-level coarse signals rather than precise facility-location effects.
